-- allium: 3
-- The consumer boundary for a database served by the default or Multi driver.
-- Multi deployment registration uses an internal journal stream. Its writer reserves the name and incarnation before provisioning the data stream.
-- The writer orders readiness after observing the selected data stream and key authority. Nodes attach the ready incarnation and recover it on restart.
-- Flight administration requires an installed deployment authorizer and a verified credential. The authority sees both names for restore and fork.
-- Multi deletion first orders a terminal in the settled deployment stream. The catalog retains the incarnation, terminal, key generation and immutable retirement cutoff.
-- Key destruction and permanent journal deletion complete before the catalog records and authenticates erasure evidence.
-- A retirement with live dependent pins or unverified independent custody keys cannot close the deployment.
-- A private Multi custody transfer selects one source pin, source cut, destination incarnation and ordered reservation.
-- The source pin has no deadline and the destination stays private until its original authority confirms the installed backing and both copy drains.
-- A retry preserves the exact bound reservation through readiness and can recover the provider terminal after source pin release.
-- An inherited carrier read requires the mounted original authority and the destination's held generation through delivery.

external entity ConsumerClient {
    -- A caller with credentials for the selected database.
}

external entity WireDatabase {
    -- The database incarnation selected when the call is admitted.
}

actor WireConsumer {
    identified_by: ConsumerClient
}

surface StatelessDatabase {
    facing consumer: WireConsumer
    context database: WireDatabase

    @guarantee CapabilityDiscovery
        -- A caller learns which operations and optional features the selected route can serve.
        -- An unavailable optional feature returns its name for comparison with Capabilities.
        -- A default attachment can serve guarded v1 writes over Classic effects covered by the shared main-branch fold.

    @guarantee CapturedRead
        -- Each successful query reports the basis of the database cut used for its rows.
        -- The database incarnation survives node restart and is shared by routes to the same database.
        -- A basis orders cuts within the selected database incarnation.
        -- The shared v1 encoding places the 16 incarnation bytes first, then the unsigned position as eight big-endian bytes.
        -- Applied-position drivers supply their applied position in this field.
        -- A branch query selects the requested branch and layer in the same fold that supplies transaction receipts.
        -- A Classic catalog query selects the installed replica cut as its basis.
        -- Exactly can reopen that Classic cut while it is the current published catalog.
        -- A local-layer read uses the branch's own writes at the selected cut.
        -- The temporal read driver supplies a system-time boundary as the basis position.
        -- Exactly selects its named cut.
        -- AtLeast returns a cut at or beyond its requested basis.
        -- AsOf applies a system-time ceiling to the selected cut.
        -- Where advertised, Linearizable establishes a fresh barrier within the call.

    @guarantee QueryContinuation
        -- A continuation reopens the same read basis and interpretation for the same ordered query and parameters.
        -- A successful final page has no continuation.
        -- Any authorized route to the same database incarnation can redeem a continuation while its cut and key generation are readable.
        -- A continuation binds its query, execution clock and next offset to the captured basis.
        -- Successive pages preserve the original token generation and share its retirement deadline.

    @guarantee RankedReads
        -- Text search returns matching rows ordered by descending relevance score.
        -- Vector nearest-neighbour reads return at most their requested limit ordered by ascending cosine distance.
        -- Ranking resolves rows at the query's selected cut.
        -- Bounded rank tables derive their totals from the effective candidate rows before paging.
        -- A tracked exact-cut rank with an empty window still returns its claim.
        -- RequireClearRankInput admits an unscoped tracked exact cut whose complete branch-view contributor inventory is proved empty.

    @guarantee QueryClaim
        -- A tracked query returns authenticated evidence naming its basis and an affected region that covers the query's dependencies.
        -- The same authority seals the claim and authenticates it when the writer evaluates Unchanged.
        -- The region can name rows, an index interval, a table, or the selected branch.
        -- The authority issues identical claim bytes for the same evidence under one key generation.

    @guarantee ReceiverClockObservation
        -- Where advertised, the first page includes a sample from the installed receiver clock when requested.
        -- The authenticated observation binds the sample, selected cut, query fingerprint and Flight session.
        -- The query trailer reports the selected system time separately.

    @guarantee OrderedIndexCatalog
        -- An ordered index declaration preserves its columns in the order sent by the caller.
        -- Query of xt.ordered_indexes reads the definitions present at its selected applied cut.
        -- A binary or text range guard can name a single-column index, and an unbounded count can name a compound index.

    @guarantee RankedIndexCatalog
        -- A ranked declaration binds its index name, ordered columns, and kind-specific options at the applied transaction position.
        -- Query of xt.ranked_indexes reads the definitions present at its selected cut and their serving mode and artefact readiness.
        -- Search declarations also supply analyzer and history definitions to the search accelerator.
        -- A declaration on an exact-SQL route reports artefacts_ready false for a physical index that the route cannot prove.

    @guarantee CountConstraintCatalog
        -- A count range limits one ordered index interval.
        -- A grouped count limits every qualifying binary key or key prefix at the constraint's valid time.
        -- Binary predicates select qualifying rows by equality on the named columns.
        -- The writer checks the final state of the selected arm before committing its effects.
        -- An unrestricted Query reads the installed typed definitions in xt.count_constraints at its selected cut.

    @guarantee TransactionRelativeValidTime
        -- Put and Delete on unlabelled tables may name a valid-time endpoint as a signed offset from the transaction time.
        -- The writer resolves both endpoints against the receipt's system time and records the resolved interval.
        -- Overflow, a reversed interval and an empty interval fail before any row effect is applied.

    @guarantee WrittenBasis
        -- Where advertised, a caller can explicitly select _rtdb_written_basis for a visible row version.
        -- It names the row version rather than the selected query cut and can be supplied to a Version guard.
        -- A Classic written basis keeps the transaction identity preserved by the resolved record or the retained event position.
        -- An imported legacy row without transaction provenance returns a null written basis.
        -- For a scoped query, the returned basis uses the reader's projection domain.

    @guarantee TokenAuthority
        -- All routes to an incarnation use the same wrapped token key for each published generation.
        -- New queries use the latest published generation.
        -- An earlier generation remains usable until its retirement deadline while the selected view is readable.
        -- Redeeming a token whose generation is erased returns erased-under-view.

    @guarantee TransactionReceipt
        -- A route that advertises writes returns its selected outcome with the applied basis and system time.
        -- A matching committed request returns the stored receipt for its key.
        -- The idempotency key within its receipt namespace identifies the request.
        -- The writer records its admission time, the complete canonical request and the selected outcome with resolved effects.
        -- Followers apply the recorded effects. Checkpoints preserve the namespaced key, request digest and outcome for retries.
        -- Validation evaluates requirements and guards at a fresh basis and leaves the applied position unchanged.

    @guarantee TransactionRequirements
        -- A write route that supports requirements advertises transact.requires.
        -- TransactRequest carries the ordered requires list as repeated Guard at protobuf field 7.
        -- Each requirement is a precondition on the original state.
        -- Passing all requirements allows guards to select an arm.
        -- The first failed requirement selects a Refused receipt with category conflict and code rtdb/requirement-failed.
        -- The refusal carries only the zero-based requirement index in its message and applies no row or catalog effects.
        -- A request with operations in either arm records that receipt for replay and TxStatus.
        -- A scoped receipt carries the basis and system time of the admitted projection.

    @guarantee PhysicalErasure
        -- A route that supports physical erasure advertises erasure.
        -- An applied erase removes row values from the clean checkpoint and orders retirement of the preceding journal and checkpoint generation.
        -- Default requires evidence for the source prefix, replica prefix and every prior recovery dependency.
        -- The clean native recovery cut binds the applied source position, its replica boundary and the exact uploaded block digest.
        -- The selected native checkpoint authenticates under the successor generation and retains the pending receipt at the erased basis.
        -- The Default writer holds later source decisions while it rewrites the physical main fold at that cut.
        -- The sealed shared image binds the rewritten table blocks and trie bodies to the exact recovery boundary.
        -- A term-admitted upload fixes the selected checkpoint before the writer records the original retirement deadline.
        -- A replacement recovers that deadline from the replica suffix before continuing retirement.
        -- The receipt carries the unreadable-by deadline after the key authority has durably armed it.
        -- A pending erasure receipt stays private to the fold until retirement is armed.
        -- Replayed erase receipts preserve that deadline after trim.
        -- Previously opened query and change streams verify their selected generation at each frame handoff.
        -- A Default view reopened from a sealed checkpoint keeps the authenticated generation of that image through frame delivery.

    @guarantee ProtectedPlacement
        -- A route that serves row custody orders original placement, reservation, activation and revocation in the same database fold.
        -- A reservation binds the exact original and reviewed source occurrences to fresh destination and link identities, a valid target interval and the reviewed carrier and link bytes.
        -- Only the ordered write at the reserved target can activate the destination, and its basis, within-transaction ordinal and encrypted envelope become part of the occurrence commitment.
        -- A same-database branch inherits the original occurrence through its fork basis.
        -- The inventory includes inactive reservations and active occurrences, so a new reservation or activation invalidates an earlier revocation plan.
        -- Revocation permanently closes placement and release admission.
        -- An exact retry can recover the original receipt even after its custody key has been destroyed.
        -- Scoped readers receive no placement or release inventory.

    @guarantee ProtectedResponseRelease
        -- The installed original carrier journal authenticates the exact attachment, original writer, response part, admitted occurrence and immutable cutoff.
        -- The original writer and carrier receipt are observed independently and verified by exact request key, basis and system time on the original attachment.
        -- Default verifies receipt installation in the canonical v1 fold and validates the exact generation held by the protected view.
        -- The carrier receipt selects the admitted occurrence and its commitment is retained in the release registration.
        -- The response release command checks the observed writer version in the same order as the hold or terminal.
        -- The writer version must own both its configured valid-time point and the current valid-time point at the ordered decision.
        -- An authenticated host mount pins the original writer and provider to the live writable database attachment.
        -- The cross-process provider response authenticates its exact challenge, operation, authority and payload with the configured registry signing key.
        -- The configured original owner may request a sealed read proof bound to its authenticated session, attachment mount, selected basis and key generation.
        -- A private release preflight binds the exact proof list, full contributor inventory and frame commitment to the independently observed original response part.
        -- Removing the mount or detaching its database closes new handoffs and preserves already ordered release evidence.
        -- The response owner holds every group and occurrence in its authenticated contributor inventory through delivery.
        -- A native ranked capture retains protected inputs before ranking or slicing, including inherited and shadowing versions.
        -- Before enqueue the mounted owner rechecks the exact frame commitment, complete promoted inventory, attachment, selected key generation and immutable deadline.
        -- The ordered release fold serializes private registration, promotion, whole-part admission, cancellation and disposal with revocation.
        -- Whole-part admission binds one frame and the complete promoted contributor inventory under an immutable cutoff and requires every contributor's group to be live.
        -- A transport may enqueue once through the original pending reservation using its fresh admission and unexpired cutoff.
        -- A retained admission replay is available to recovery and grants no enqueue authority.
        -- Every admitted part stays in each contributing group's drain until the exact original carrier terminal, including across loss of a reply or process restart.
        -- Only private cancellation or authenticated durable transport disposal terminates an admitted release.
        -- Recovery retains the original terminal decision and evidence through checkpoint and trim.
        -- A revoked group's release holds drain before key destruction.
        -- A route advertises row custody once its protected readers, release adapters and key enforcement support these guarantees.

    @guarantee ChangesCoverage
        -- Changes reports its covered interval even when there are no changes for the selected tables.
        -- An applied-position route takes its coverage from the driver's retained effect history.
        -- After opening a default branch checkpoint, the retained change interval starts at the checkpoint's basis.
        -- A reported erasure has a key when keys are requested and omits both row images.
        -- Default and Multi report the previous image at a closure and the new image at an opening.
        -- A returned page carries whole system-time groups.
        -- An expired starting cut returns the cursor-expired code.
        -- A waiting call returns after a relevant change, complete coverage of its requested interval or expiry of its wait.

    @guarantee ChangesProgress
        -- Where advertised, progress-only Changes returns the schema and coverage trailer for the request.
        -- The same table admission, group limits, wait and coverage rules apply to a progress-only request.

    @guarantee ScopedVisibilityCapability
        -- Scoped visibility is a target capability specified by ScopedRowVisibility in allium/rtdb-multi.allium.
        -- A route without an applied scoped projection omits row-visibility from Capabilities.
        -- An OIDC label scope binds its credential to the verified database name and incarnation.
        -- A scoped credential requires a route that enforces that scope for every admitted operation.
        -- The initial scoped serving path selects merged main and uses a logical system-time coordinate.
        -- Default mode and Multi use the same scoped projection for Query, Changes, and transaction receipts.
        -- Changes waits for progress observable by the selected scope or the requested deadline.
        -- Put rows can supply literal signed-microsecond valid-time bounds in _valid_from and _valid_to.

    @guarantee ChangesImages
        -- Changes image columns carry individual Arrow IPC streams by default.
        -- A route advertising changes.images.stream-v1 accepts an explicit request for shared image streams and acknowledges the selected representation in the response.
        -- In the shared representation each present image column carries one Arrow IPC stream in its first cell, with one image per result row in that batch's order.
        -- Image selection, change ordering and covered intervals apply equally to both representations.
}
